{"id":"277b566f-db1d-411c-8e04-860837b1dbd1","object":"chat.completion","created":1784825031,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not payload or 'segments' not in payload or 'budget' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case: missing payload fields\\\")\\n    segments_data = payload['segments']\\n    budget = payload['budget']\\n    if not isinstance(budget, int) or budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n    n = len(segments_data)\\n    segs = []\\n    total_min = 0\\n    total_cap = 0\\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments_data):\\n        name = seg['name']\\n        importance = seg['importance']\\n        minimum = seg['minimum']\\n        capacity = seg['capacity']\\n        if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in (importance, minimum, capacity)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Segment fields must be integers\\\")\\n        if minimum < 0 or capacity < 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Minimum and capacity must be non-negative\\\")\\n        if minimum > capacity:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Segment {name}: minimum > capacity\\\")\\n        total_min += minimum\\n        total_cap += capacity\\n        segs.append({\\n            'name': name,\\n            'importance': importance,\\n            'minimum': minimum,\\n            'capacity': capacity,\\n            'allocated': minimum,\\n            'index': i\\n        })\\n    if budget < total_min:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Budget {budget} less than total minimum {total_min}\\\")\\n    if budget > total_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Budget {budget} exceeds total capacity {total_cap}\\\")\\n    remaining = budget - total_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_ratio = -1.0\\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segs):\\n            if seg['allocated'] >= seg['capacity']:\\n                continue\\n            ratio = seg['importance'] / (seg['allocated'] + 1)\\n            if ratio > best_ratio:\\n                best_ratio = ratio\\n                best_idx = i\\n            elif ratio == best_ratio and best_idx != -1:\\n                if seg['index'] < segs[best_idx]['index']:\\n                    best_idx = i\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Unexpected: cannot allocate more\\\")\\n        segs[best_idx]['allocated'] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    allocations = {seg['name']: seg['allocated'] for seg in segs}\\n    segment_order = [seg['name'] for seg in segs]\\n    total_allocated = sum(seg['allocated'] for seg in segs)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"allocations\\\": allocations,\\n        \\\"segment_order\\\": segment_order,\\n        \\\"total\\\": total_allocated\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as Python source code defining a function solve(case). The problem is a budget allocation problem: given segments with importance, minimum, capacity, and a budget. Allocate exactly budget units (tokens) to segments, each at least minimum and at most capacity. If budget is less than sum of minimums or greater than total capacity, raise ValueError. The allocation method: start with each segment at minimum, then repeatedly allocate remaining budget one token at a time to the segment that maximizes importance divided by (next allocated position). That is, for segment i, after allocating k_i tokens (including minimum), the next token's \"value\" is importance_i / (k_i + 1). Actually, the description: \"While budget remains, give one token to the segment maximizing importance divided by its next allocated position.\" So if current allocated tokens = x, next position is x+1, so ratio = importance / (x+1). Maximize that. Ties broken by original segment order (index order). Return allocations as dict mapping segment name to allocated tokens, also the name mapping (maybe just the list of segment names), and conserved total (sum of allocations). The output should be JSON-serializable. The function must not mutate case, use only standard library.\n\nWe need to write full Python code. The case payload has segments list and budget. Each segment has name, importance, minimum, capacity. We'll parse and validate.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Check case has 'payload' with 'segments' and 'budget'. Validate types.\n2. For each segment, check that minimum <= capacity, and importance >=0 (though not specified, but safe). Also check that sum of minimums <= budget <= sum of capacities. Otherwise raise ValueError.\n3. Initialize allocations: dict or list of allocated counts per segment, start with minimum.\n4. remaining = budget - sum of minimums.\n5. While remaining > 0:\n   - For each segment, compute ratio = importance / (allocated + 1). But if allocated already equals capacity, skip (cannot allocate more). Actually, if allocated >= capacity, cannot allocate. So if allocated >= capacity, set ratio to -inf or skip.\n   - Find segment with max ratio. In case of tie, pick the one with smallest original index.\n   - Add 1 to that segment's allocated, decrement remaining.\n6. Return result: dict with keys:\n   - allocations: dict mapping name to allocated count (int)\n   - total_allocated: sum of allocations (should equal budget)\n   - segments: maybe original order list of names? The description says \"name mapping\" but unclear. Better to include list of segment names in order? Or just return allocations and total. The candidate artifact says \"Return allocations, the name mapping, and the conserved total.\" So likely return an object with \"allocations\" (dict), \"segment_order\" (list of names), \"total\" (int). But also \"name mapping\" might be just list of names. We'll include \"segment_order\": [names in original order].\n   - Also include \"budget_used\": total allocated.\n   - Also maybe \"case_id\" from case? Not specified. But the output should be JSON-serializable object. The function should return a dict.\n\nWe'll strictly follow: return a dict with keys \"allocations\", \"segment_order\", \"total\". And also include \"budget\" from case to show it's conserved? But that's input. The conserved total is sum allocations. So we'll output that.\n\nMake sure to not mutate case: we won't modify any input fields.\n\nEdge cases: If budget exactly equals sum of minimums, allocations are just minimums. If budget equals total capacity, allocate all to capacity. If budget is outside, raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll use integer arithmetic; importance is int, ratio is float. Use division: importance / (allocated+1). Use Python's division.\n\nImplementation details: we'll store segments in a list of dicts with index. Use while loop.\n\nOne more thing: The problem mentions \"coarse_budget_controller\" and \"solve(case)\". So the function is solve.\n\nWrite as a single function with docstring? Not necessary but fine.\n\nNow produce JSON string with escaped characters. The implementation value must be Python source code as a string. We'll write the code, escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's craft the code.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get('payload')\n    if not payload or 'segments' not in payload or 'budget' not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing payload fields\")\n    segments_data = payload['segments']\n    budget = payload['budget']\n    if not isinstance(budget, int) or budget < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget must be non-negative integer\")\n    n = len(segments_data)\n    # Validate each segment\n    segs = []\n    total_min = 0\n    total_cap = 0\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments_data):\n        name = seg['name']\n        importance = seg['importance']\n        minimum = seg['minimum']\n        capacity = seg['capacity']\n        if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in (importance, minimum, capacity)):\n            raise ValueError(\"Segment fields must be integers\")\n        if minimum < 0 or capacity < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Minimum and capacity must be non-negative\")\n        if minimum > capacity:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Segment {name}: minimum > capacity\")\n        total_min += minimum\n        total_cap += capacity\n        segs.append({\n            'name': name,\n            'importance': importance,\n            'minimum': minimum,\n            'capacity': capacity,\n            'allocated': minimum,\n            'index': i\n        })\n    if budget < total_min:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Budget {budget} less than total minimum {total_min}\")\n    if budget > total_cap:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Budget {budget} exceeds total capacity {total_cap}\")\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n    # Allocate remaining tokens\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_ratio = -1.0\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segs):\n            if seg['allocated'] >= seg['capacity']:\n                continue\n            ratio = seg['importance'] / (seg['allocated'] + 1)\n            if ratio > best_ratio:\n                best_ratio = ratio\n                best_idx = i\n            elif ratio == best_ratio and best_idx != -1:\n                # tie break by original order (index)\n                if seg['index'] < segs[best_idx]['index']:\n                    best_idx = i\n        if best_idx == -1:\n            # should not happen if budget <= total_cap, but safety\n            raise ValueError(\"Unexpected: cannot allocate more\")\n        segs[best_idx]['allocated'] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    # Build result\n    allocations = {seg['name']: seg['allocated'] for seg in segs}\n    segment_order = [seg['name'] for seg in segs]\n    total_allocated = sum(seg['allocated'] for seg in segs)\n    return {\n        \"allocations\": allocations,\n        \"segment_order\": segment_order,\n        \"total\": total_allocated\n    }\n\nThat's the code. Ensure it returns exactly the required fields. Also handle case where no remaining (budget = total_min). Then while loop doesn't execute.\n\nNow produce JSON. The source string must be escaped. We'll build a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the code as a string.\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid: double quotes, escape backslashes and newlines. We'll manually craft or use a tool mentally.\n\nWrite the code as a single line with \\n for newlines and \\\" for quotes inside the string. But easier to write the code in a multi-line string and then escape. We'll produce the final JSON.\n\nFinal answer: a JSON object with one key \"implementation\" and value the Python source string.\n\nLet's construct."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1150,"completion_tokens":2405,"total_tokens":3555,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1727},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":126},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}